1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. h4 $5 {Fedoseev is a creative player who tends to be very
successful with his unconventional play, that usually starts right from the
opening. The idea of White's move is obvious: to push the pawn to h5 at some
point. Is it correct $2 Of course not. At this point in the game it's more
important to develop the pieces and it's not even clear when h4-h5 is really
interesting and when it simply loses a pawn. Most importantly, however, and I
imagine it was played for this reason, it makes both players start thinking with
their own heads.} 3... d6 {There are many alternatives at every move in this
opening. I'll simply mention some of them.} (3... Bg7) (3... c5) (3... h5) 4.
Nc3 Nbd7 {Simple development in King's Indian style.} 5. Qc2 {Already a novelty
on move five. I really enjoy games like this. This is another move whose idea is
basically just to leave common sense behind: it's hard to say why this can be
better than simply playing the pawn to e4.} (5. e4) 5... c5 6. d5 a6 {Salem is
not hiding his intention: he wants to sac a pawn with b7-b5, an idea known from
the Benko Gambit. Naturally, there are alternatives to this plan.} (6... Ne5
{One of the problems with the h4-move is that the knight is relatively safe now
on e5, since f2-f4 won't be possible anytime soon. With the pawn on h3, the
knight wouldn't be feeling so happy.}) (6... Bg7 {Simple development.}) (6... h5
{Just in case Black doesn't want to worry about h4-h5 anymore.}) 7. e4 (7. a4
{is possible, preventing Black's b-pawn from advancing. But with the pawns on a4
and h4, where is White's king going $2 Although it certainly isn't going to the
queenside after b7-b5 as well.} 7... Ne5) 7... b5 {After saying \"A\" you have
to say \"B\".} 8. cxb5 Bg7 9. bxa6 {This move helps Black's counterplay. It's
more advisable to develop the knight to f3 instead and wait for Black to capture
on b5.} (9. Nf3) 9... O-O (9... Qa5 {is also possible, with good compensation
after} 10. Bd2 Bxa6) 10. h5 $6 {Fedoseev cannot resist a street fight. This move
is probably not the best, but it gives him the type of game he likes.} (10. Nf3)
10... Nxh5 11. g4 Nhf6 12. g5 {This is the point of the sacrifice. If the knight
goes to h5, then White can play Be2, take it, and start an attack. If it goes to
e8, White tries to mate on the h-file.} 12... Nh5 {The most natural move.}
(12... Ne8 {is ugly, but not so bad.} 13. f4 {Clearing the path for the queen.}
13... Bxa6 14. Bxa6 Rxa6 15. Nf3 {with compensation in a double-edged position.}
(15. Qh2 $6 {is still not so dangerous:} 15... h5 16. gxh6 Bxc3+ 17. bxc3 Ra4
{Black's king is quite safe, unlike White's.})) 13. Be2 Bxa6 14. Bxh5 gxh5 15.
f4 $1 {Necessary to prevent the knight from going to e5 and to prepare Qh2.} (15.
Rxh5 $2 Ne5 {is clearly good for Black.}) 15... e6 {Opening the center is a good
idea, since White's king is not safe either. There's another way to try this
plan:} (15... f5 $5 16. gxf6 (16. exf5 {is weaker:} 16... Qe8 {with the idea of
Qf7 and a good position for Black.} (16... Bxc3+ {first is also possible.}))
16... Rxf6 {Now a possible continuation is:} (16... Nxf6 {is also good.}) 17.
Rxh5 Qf8 18. Nf3 Rxf4 $5 19. Bxf4 Qxf4 {with excellent compensation for Black
with powerful bishops cutting the board.}) 16. Nge2 {Preparing Ng3 and maybe
taking the h5-pawn with the knight.} 16... Qe8 $5 {Keeping one eye on h5 and the
other on e1.} 17. dxe6 fxe6 18. Ng3 Ne5 $5 {A fantastic move: Salem also loves
crazy positions and playing with the initiative. Both players were probably very
happy at this point. There are several alternatives and this position can be
analyzed for hours.} (18... d5 19. Nxh5 {with unclear play.}) (18... Bd4 $5 19.
Nxh5 Ne5 $5 {A similar idea to the game.} 20. fxe5 Rf1+ (20... Qxh5 {is also
possible:} 21. Rxh5 Rf1+ 22. Kd2 Rf2+ 23. Ne2 (23. Kd1 Rf1+ {is a draw.}) 23...
Bxe2 24. Ke1 Bxh5 25. Qxf2 Bxf2+ 26. Kxf2 d5 27. exd5 exd5 {The endgame likely
ends in a draw.}) 21. Rxf1 Qxh5 {Black is a rook down but has some serious
compensation. Somehow it seems that the game is balanced after} 22. Ne2) 19.
fxe5 Bxe5 20. Qg2 {The only move. White needs to protect the knight and
